<EndToEnd />

## Two loops

<At levels="simple">

Two loops run at once. Inside, the model writes one token at a time until it's done. Outside, the
harness checks what the model wrote, runs any tool it asked for, adds the result, and calls the
model again. Here the harness called the model twice: once to search for a nursery rhyme, and once
to answer.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Two loops run at once. The inner one is the model's: run once, pick one token, add it, and run
again, until it writes an end-of-turn token. The outer one is the harness's: read what the model
wrote, and if it asked for a tool, run it, add the result, and call the model again. Here the outer
loop ran twice: once to ask for a nursery rhyme search, once to answer with what came back.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

Everything in both loops is plain text in one prompt: your message, the instructions, and the
tool's result. That's why tool results can't be trusted blindly.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Everything that passes through both loops is plain text. Your message, the system prompt, the tool's
result, and the model's scratch work all end up as tokens in one prompt. That is why reusing the
unchanged start saves so much work, and why a tool result has to be treated as untrusted.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Counting the runs

The first call read the 400-token prompt in one pass, then wrote its 21-token tool call one token
per run until the harness stopped it at `</tool_call>`. The second call's prompt began with all
400 tokens of the first, so with prompt caching only the 93 new ones needed reading: 493 tokens of
work instead of 893 (<ChapterRef to="caching" />). It then wrote its 59-token reply and an
end-of-turn token, again one run each. That's about 80 runs of the model for one short message, and
thanks to the KV cache each run worked through just one new token.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Sending a message runs the harness first, then the model once per token, then the harness again.
- The model's loop adds one token per run; the harness's loop adds one tool result per call.
- Everything the model sees, including tool results, is one stream of tokens in a single prompt.
- Other kinds of models swap one stage and keep the rest of the order.

<At levels="deep">

- Two calls, about 80 model runs: with caching, the second call read only the 93 new tokens.

</At>
</RecapCard>

